{"type": "say", "text": "I agree with Samuel's recommendation on keeping the force-majeure notice focused on the required elements — the force-majeure event, the affected facilities, and the expected duration — without venturing into allocation methodology. That aligns with the notice exhibit requirements I confirmed: we need to identify the affected facilities and send by both email and overnight courier within five business days. Including allocation percentages would go beyond what the notice requires and could create the kind of exposure Samuel is concerned about. On the parallel track, I'd recommend we draft the force-majeure notice now using Mei's preliminary facility identification, with a placeholder pending her final confirmation. That way, once the inventory reconciliation is complete, we can do a final check and send immediately by both email and overnight courier. I do want to flag one thing for the group: the five-business-day clock — I haven't yet confirmed when Day One falls for purposes of that calculation, so we should pin down the triggering date to make sure we're not already consuming part of the window. Elena, I'd recommend we authorize Samuel to send the carrier notice today as he proposed, and in parallel I'll have the force-majeure notice draft ready for review with Mei's final confirmation built in as the last step before sending."}
